insert into public.tips (id, unit_id, title_en, body_md, status) values
  ('4a694bf1-31bd-5bed-856d-62d6bdf86ec7', '2fa4826d-6276-5a6f-b7a5-e3c2e6dc4f2d', 'The past, one person at a time', 'For anything finished, Buenos Aires uses one past: *laburé* (I worked), *laburaste* (you worked), *laburó* (he/she worked). The stress is what tells *laburo* (I work) from *laburó* (he worked) — the accent is not decoration.', 'draft'),
  ('d1b02a61-df35-5be3-af56-85efe6e071b7', '2fa4826d-6276-5a6f-b7a5-e3c2e6dc4f2d', 'Not "he trabajado"', 'Spain says *hoy he trabajado*; a porteño says **hoy laburé**. Even for today, even for something that just happened: **¿Llegaste?** — *Did you get here?*', 'draft'),
  ('f3006b2f-a540-5da1-90a0-3fcad0038181', 'b60b2d1f-25dc-5ada-9b93-a0afe7bd9a37', '-er and -ir share one past', '*Comer* and *salir* take the same endings: **comí, comiste, comió**; **salí, saliste, salió**. For *nosotros* the past looks like the present — *salimos* — and *anoche* or *ayer* says which one it is.', 'draft'),
  ('41ce14aa-729b-54bd-999f-93b03dc71add', 'b60b2d1f-25dc-5ada-9b93-a0afe7bd9a37', 'Recién', '**Recién llegué** — *I just got here*. A porteño uses *recién* with the plain past where English says *just*.', 'draft'),
  ('a4151119-a7fd-5b3e-9b83-3fbadc5e46db', 'e89d1870-c4d1-5692-923d-5e357ae8218e', 'Fui: went, and was', '*Ir* and *ser* share their past: **fui, fuiste, fue**. *Fui a la cancha* — *I went to the stadium*; *fue bárbaro* — *it was great*. The rest of the sentence tells which.', 'draft'),
  ('f1cfa2ef-6b5e-57fc-86fc-c107e2fe7bb4', 'e89d1870-c4d1-5692-923d-5e357ae8218e', '¿Cómo te fue?', 'The porteño *how did it go?* is **¿Cómo te fue?**, and the answer is **Me fue bien** or **Me fue re mal**.', 'draft'),
  ('bbaf9a50-b716-51ad-9001-602ef86b1a04', 'f42f6608-6d16-5d0a-bd3f-896cd5fb4d07', 'The irregular ones', 'The verbs you use most have their own past: **hice** (I did), **vi** (I saw), **dije** (I said), **vine** (I came), **pude** (I could). No accent on any of them.', 'draft'),
  ('9e470499-223a-5382-8475-2b677a8e1fde', 'f42f6608-6d16-5d0a-bd3f-896cd5fb4d07', '¿Viste?', '*¿Viste?* is also just *you saw*: **¿Viste el partido?** — *Did you see the game?* — the same word porteños drop into every story to mean *you know?*', 'draft'),
  ('a619c661-a610-55a6-a9b7-1b1f8185673c', 'fd0afb5a-c86b-5339-b28f-31f5431f400a', 'Más… que, menos… que', '**Más caro que**, **menos lejos que** — *more expensive than*, *less far than*. **Tan lindo como** is *as nice as*. *Better* and *worse* have their own words: **mejor**, **peor**.', 'draft'),
  ('e3774fe4-ab31-5852-860c-b8663022162f', 'fd0afb5a-c86b-5339-b28f-31f5431f400a', 'Older and younger', 'A porteño''s older sister is *mi hermana **más grande***, the younger one *mi hermano **más chico***. And *-ísimo* turns anything up: **riquísimo**, **carísimo**.', 'draft'),
  ('e09dc1af-b15b-5bb4-a301-1cf6109dfb09', '14b391bd-5db8-50a4-a988-428361e7752a', 'Doler works like gustar', 'The thing that hurts is the subject: **me duele la cabeza** — *my head hurts*, **me duelen los pies** — *my feet hurt*. The *me* says whose, so Spanish uses *la*, not *mi*.', 'draft'),
  ('d389c9c5-149a-57c8-b192-f1851fa1b7df', '14b391bd-5db8-50a4-a988-428361e7752a', 'At the doctor''s', 'You ask for a **turno** (an appointment); the **guardia** is the emergency room. **Estoy engripado** — *I''ve got the flu* — is what gets you the day off.', 'draft'),
  ('3ad63ad3-5180-58b1-ac1e-ea3eb60e3779', '9230420b-8a48-5c4b-b9d9-b7dc3c12bb5d', 'Lo, la, los, las', '**¿Tenés la tarjeta? Sí, la tengo.** The pronoun stands for the thing and goes before the verb: *lo* and *los* for masculine words, *la* and *las* for feminine ones.', 'draft'),
  ('81cf4a55-244b-58cc-a47c-0b1e1ef25493', '9230420b-8a48-5c4b-b9d9-b7dc3c12bb5d', 'Stuck onto a command', 'With a vos command the pronoun joins the end, and there is no accent: **llamame**, **mandame un audio**, **dame**, **prestame**. *Le* and *les* are *to him, to her, to them*.', 'draft'),
  ('35b971dd-87ee-5d40-9e55-ceb3b73004bc', 'c72176ae-a8de-5a47-9b37-cf9bdf84de08', 'Me toca', '**Me toca a mí** — *it''s my turn*. **¿A quién le toca?** — *whose turn is it?* The chore follows as it is: **te toca lavar los platos**.', 'draft'),
  ('8f8bb283-35a4-5157-9b3c-f50aae639ff9', 'c72176ae-a8de-5a47-9b37-cf9bdf84de08', 'Las compras', '**Hacer las compras** is doing the food shopping; **sacar la basura** is taking the rubbish out.', 'draft'),
  ('e6e2d7cb-a37e-547a-b841-bd24476880df', '7c33a900-8b22-5a9e-a34e-7c274e6a21b0', 'Micro, not colectivo', 'The *colectivo* crosses the city; to go to another city you take the **micro** (the coach). You buy the **pasaje** at the terminal or online.', 'draft'),
  ('bd699582-34b8-5eff-8b28-4010c536d54e', '7c33a900-8b22-5a9e-a34e-7c274e6a21b0', 'Summer is in January', 'Porteños go on **vacaciones** in January and February, and *la costa* — Mar del Plata, Pinamar — is where half of Buenos Aires ends up.', 'draft'),
  ('c3ebca78-c01d-5635-ae32-235914cc3de0', '07ff3e13-7b95-55ab-aab7-ac48151e0d8c', 'How a porteño tells it', '**Contame** opens it; **de repente** is the twist, **por suerte** the relief, **al final** the end. **Igual** — *anyway* — goes almost anywhere, and **o sea** is *I mean*.', 'draft'),
  ('c36061ee-ffa6-50e3-a2e5-06e46a8619e6', '07ff3e13-7b95-55ab-aab7-ac48151e0d8c', '¡Qué garrón!', 'When the story ends badly, the answer is **¡Qué garrón!** — *what a drag!* Informal, and everywhere.', 'draft')
